The == operator is used for comparing objects of the same class or instance. It returns true if two objects have the same value at the same time, regardless of their type. For example:

```
String str1 = "Hello";
String str2 = "Hello";

if (str1.equals(str2)) {
    System.out.println("Strings are equal");
}
```

In this case, both `str1` and `str2` contain the string "Hello", so they are considered equal by the == operator.

On the other hand, the != operator is used for comparing objects of different classes or instances. It returns true only if two objects have different values at the same time. For example:

```
String str1 = new String("Hello");
String str2 = new String("Hello");

if (!str1.equals(str2)) {
    System.out.println("Strings are not equal");
}
```

In this case, `str1` and `str2` contain the same string, but they are from different classes, so they are considered unequal by the != operator.

So, while == is useful when you want to check if two objects have the same value at the same time, it can lead to bugs if you use it incorrectly. On the other hand, != is more specific and helps you distinguish between objects of different classes or instances.

